-- title:
--   Uniform twelve-step Syracuse descent on 1602 fifteen-branch progressions modulo $2^{20}$
-- statement:
--   Let $T(n)$ be the odd part of $3n+1$ (the Syracuse map, `syracuseStep` on the platform), and let $T^{12}$ denote twelve successive applications. Let $R$ be the explicit list of 1602 distinct residues modulo $2^{20}=1048576$ enumerated in the formal statement. For every natural number $n$,
--
--   $$
--   n \bmod 1048576 \in R \quad\Longrightarrow\quad T^{12}(n) < n .
--   $$
--
--   The statement is uniform over 1602 infinite arithmetic progressions; it is not a check of least representatives. Every listed residue $r$ satisfies $r\equiv 15 \pmod{16}$ and lies in a class left open by the [fifteen-branch residual descent theorem modulo $2^{18}$](https://prove2.me/theorems/db05bdcc-6d11-411b-9a9b-4421004e2b55). That residual consists of 4723 classes modulo $2^{18}$, hence $4\cdot 4723 = 18892$ classes modulo $2^{20}$, and $R$ is the set of those lifts on which the first twelve Syracuse steps divide by exactly $2^{20}$ in total. Since $3^{12}=531441<2^{20}$, the twelfth iterate on such a class is an affine function of $n$ with slope $3^{12}/2^{20}<1$.
--
--   The theorem removes $1602/18892\approx 8.48\%$ of the density of the fifteen-branch residual and is the certified half of its refinement from modulus $2^{18}$ to $2^{20}$. No class of that residual is certifiable at modulus $2^{19}$.
--
--   **Formalization Note.** The residue table is a literal `List ℕ`. The recursion-depth option only permits elaboration of the long literal.
-- source:
--   Original computation of this contribution (explicit certified refinement of https://prove2.me/theorems/db05bdcc-6d11-411b-9a9b-4421004e2b55 from modulus 2^18 to 2^20; the residue table is computed, not quoted from the literature). Exact Syracuse definition https://prove2.me/theorems/2d5fcb43-85b2-4d75-beb8-3e236e66eac3. Method and packaging follow the modulus-2^18 affine-trace certificates of syracuse_descent_progressions_mod262144, https://prove2.me/theorems/b4cf0c47-c078-4235-b853-32b5a518517e, and the twentyseven-branch analogue syracuse_descent_twentyseven_mod32_mod1048576_step12. Background: R. Terras, A stopping time problem on the positive integers, Acta Arith. 30 (1976), 241-252 (stopping time determined by the residue modulo a power of two); J. C. Lagarias, The 3x+1 Problem and Its Generalizations, Amer. Math. Monthly 92 (1985), Section 2, https://websites.umich.edu/~lagarias/3x%2B1.html.

